<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan Kudarat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1581–<a href="/years/1671">1671</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Dipatuan Kudarat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Kudarat was ruler of the Sultanate of Maguindanao on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> from about 1619 to 1671. He united much of Mindanao, resisted Spanish expansion and survived the expedition of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-hurtado-de-corcuera">Sebastián Hurtado de Corcuera</a>, who took his fortress at Lamitan in 1637. He later made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on equal terms, and he is honoured as a national hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kudarat was a direct descendant of Shariff Kabungsuwan, a Malay-Arab noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> who in tradition brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>. His name joins the Malay title di-Pertuan, ruler, literally the one who has been made to rule, with Kudarat, from the Arabic qudrat, power, which in Maguindanaon was sometimes pronounced Kurlat. When Sultan Laut Buisan died, custom expected his successor to come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a>, but Kudarat lobbied the datus of Maguindanao and Lanao for himself. The displeasure of the Lanao datus led Lanao to break away and form the Lanao sultanates, and about the same time Kudarat made an armistice with the rajas of Buayan to prevent raids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1619–1621 Kudarat allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, selling it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and slaves captured from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a>, and he played the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and the Spaniards off against each other as both sought access to local ports and the spice trade. When the Spanish expedition of 1637 reached his strongly fortified position at Lamitan, the Spaniards took 8 bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, 27 lantaka or culverins and 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, but Kudarat escaped to the mountains and gathered his strength again.</p><p>In 1639, when the Spaniards invaded the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranao-people">Maranaos</a>, he went to confer with the datus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a>. A Spanish ambassador recorded his appeal to them not to sell themselves into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to toil for foreigners, as he said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalogs</a> and Visayans had. Within months the Spaniards withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> and did not venture into inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> again. By the end of 1639 Kudarat had also reached an understanding with Datu Maputi for a united front against them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 June 1645 Governor-General Diego Fajardo Chacón signed a treaty with Kudarat that let Spanish missionaries minister to the Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, allowed a church to be built and opened trade in the sultan's territories. Kudarat died in 1671, aged 89 or 90, at Simuay, now part of the municipality of Sultan Kudarat in Maguindanao del Norte. The Soccsksargen province of Sultan Kudarat is also named after him, and his descendants, who bear the title of datu, take part in local politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Kudarat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Kudarat</b>, reigning from about 1619 to 1671, united much of Mindanao, fought Governor Sebastian Hurtado de Corcuera in 1637 and made peace with Spain on his own terms.</blockquote><a href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maguindanao, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.13,124.3&amp;year=1639" aria-label="Maguindanao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sultan Kudarat"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5365 4392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5365 4392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.13,124.3&amp;year=1639">What was here in 1639? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
